    { name: 't', label: 'temperature (in kT units)', min: 0.3, max: 2, step: 0.1, value: 1 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 5, label: 'energy above ground (in kT units)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const heatCfg = {
  title: 'Worked example: why heat flows hot to cold, by counting',
  steps: [
    { math: '1/T = ΔS/ΔE', note: 'the statistical definition of temperature: how much entropy each extra unit of energy buys.' },
    { math: 'hot body: T_h = 400 K → 1/T_h = 0.0025 /K', note: 'a hot system is energy-rich; each further joule adds little entropy.' },
    { math: 'cold body: T_c = 300 K → 1/T_c = 0.0033 /K', note: 'a cold system is energy-hungry; a joule buys it lots of new arrangements.' },
    { math: 'move Q = 1 J from hot to cold: ΔS = Q(1/T_c − 1/T_h) = 0.0008 J/K', note: 'net entropy increases — that is why the flow happens.' },
    { math: 'reverse it: ΔS = −0.0008 J/K', note: 'entropy would fall: not impossible, just overwhelmingly improbable. No law forbids it; statistics does.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/advanced/statistical-mechanics">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Physics lesson, and the ladder's great unifier. It assumes the
    intermediate <em>Thermal Physics</em> lesson — the laws of heat whose origins this page
    excavates — and the foundational <em>Energy and Its Conservation</em> and <em>Newton's
    Three Laws</em> lessons, whose mechanics supplies everything that is about to be
    counted. The advanced <em>Quantum Foundations</em> lesson arrives exactly where classical
    counting fails, both in the heat-capacity story and in the quantum statistics section.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define microstate and macrostate, and explain why the second law is a statement about counting rather than about force;</li>
    <li>use S = k·ln W and 1/T = ΔS/ΔE to say what entropy and temperature physically measure;</li>
    <li>read the Boltzmann factor and predict which energy states molecules occupy — from reaction rates to the atmosphere's scale height;</li>
    <li>derive heat capacities from equipartition, and locate the quantum correction where vibration freezes out;</li>
    <li>apply the Maxwell–Boltzmann tail to evaporation, chemistry and atmospheric escape.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The thermal-physics lesson gave you thermodynamics as a set of rules — energy conserved,
    entropy increases, heat flows downhill. Statistical mechanics is where those rules come
    <em>from</em>: it explains pressure, temperature and entropy as counting arguments over
    ~10²³ molecules, showing that the laws of heat are not new forces of nature but the
    statistics of ordinary mechanics applied to astronomical numbers of particles. The
    conceptual leap: give up tracking every molecule (impossible anyway) and ask instead
    how many microscopic arrangements — <strong>microstates</strong> — correspond to each
    big-picture description — a <strong>macrostate</strong> (same pressure, same
    temperature). Nature does not favour order or mess; it favours whichever macrostate has
    the most microstates, and the numbers involved make that bias effectively absolute.
  </p>
  <p>
    The field's two master results are Boltzmann's entropy formula and the Boltzmann
    factor. One defines temperature and the second law; the other governs which energy
    levels molecules occupy, why reactions speed up when heated, why air thins with
    altitude, and why your coffee cools. Both are, at bottom, the arithmetic of very large
    numbers — the same combinatorics as the number-theory lesson's factorials, weaponised.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Boltzmann factor — hotter systems can afford higher-energy states; the second curve is a colder world" widget="plotter" config={boltzCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the red curve is the Boltzmann factor at the chosen
    temperature; the grey curve is a colder world at half of it. Slide the temperature up
    and the curve's shoulder spreads rightward — higher-energy states become affordable;
    slide it down and occupation piles into the ground state. Read gaps as ratios: at an
    energy of 5kT the colder curve sits about 150 times lower than the hotter one, and that
    exponential ratio is precisely why small temperature changes have large chemical
    consequences.
  </p>

  <h2>Counting arrangements: where entropy comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the smallest example that shows the avalanche. Flip 10 coins: the
    macrostate "5 heads" has W = C(10,5) = 252 microstates; "10 heads" has exactly 1.
    Already the balanced macrostate is 252 times more likely. Now 100 coins: "50 heads"
    has W = C(100,50) ≈ 1.0 × 10²⁹ microstates versus 1 for "all heads" — and the
    distribution's width only grows like √N, so the relative fluctuations shrink. Scale
    to a gas of 10²³ molecules and the most-probable macrostate outnumber any
    near-miss by factors like 10^(10²¹): deviating from equilibrium by even one part in a
    billion is, probabilistically, a non-event. Boltzmann's insight was that entropy
    <em>is</em> this count: <strong>S = k·ln W</strong>, with k = 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K — a
    formula so important it is carved on his gravestone in Vienna. The logarithm is not
    decoration: it makes entropy additive (combine two independent systems and the
    microstates multiply, W = W₁·W₂, so entropies add) and it makes the numbers
    manageable — ln(10²³) is only about 53.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second law is now a theorem-shaped statement about counting: isolated systems
    evolve toward macrostates of maximal W because those macrostates are where
    essentially <em>all</em> the microstates live. A shuffled deck never unshuffles for
    the same reason — there is no force pushing it back, just 8 × 10⁶⁷ orderings of which
    essentially none are "sorted". Note the subtle shift this brings: the second law is
    <strong>statistical, not absolute</strong>. Microscopic dynamics is time-reversible;
    the arrow of time is bookkeeping. Fluctuations back toward lower entropy are allowed
    — measurable in small systems (bacteria-scale motors exploit them) and absurdly
    unlikely at macroscopic scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> entropy is "disorder", so tidy rooms violate it. The
    thermodynamic quantity counts microstates per macrostate; a crystal at absolute zero
    and a tidy room are both low-entropy in their everyday senses, but cleaning a room
    exports more entropy (heat, sweat, discarded packaging) than it removes — the room's
    surroundings pay with interest. Physicists increasingly prefer "spread": entropy
    measures how spread-out energy and matter are over available microstates.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> entropy is the logarithm of a count, and the second law is what
    the count makes overwhelming. Next the same bookkeeping is turned around to define
    temperature itself — not as a reading on a gauge, but as the entropy price of an extra
    joule.
  </p>

  <h2>Temperature redefined: the entropy price list</h2>
  <p>
    The definition hidden inside the second law is
    <strong>1/T = ΔS/ΔE</strong>: temperature is the reciprocal of how much entropy each
    added joule purchases. A cold system buys lots of entropy per joule; a hot one buys
    little. Heat flows from hot to cold because the cold side's entropy gain exceeds the
    hot side's loss — every transfer is a net win for the universe's W:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the zeroth law and the second law in one ledger — temperature is the exchange rate between energy and entropy" widget="equationStep" config={heatCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the cards run one joule through a ledger. The hot body's
    1/T is smaller, so losing a joule costs it little entropy, while the cold body's larger
    1/T means the same joule buys plenty — the difference, 0.0008 J/K, is the net gain that
    drives the flow. Card 5 is the punchline: reverse the transfer and the ledger goes
    negative — forbidden by no law of mechanics, only by probability. Notice that temperature
    never appears as a substance or a fluid anywhere in the argument; it is an exchange rate.
  </p>

  <p>
    This definition also explains the zeroth law (bodies in contact stop exchanging net
    heat exactly when their ΔS/ΔE match — that match <em>is</em> equal temperature) and
    delivers a genuine shock: feed energy into a system whose entropy <em>falls</em> with
    energy (possible in population-inverted systems like laser media) and 1/T is
    negative. Negative absolute temperatures are <strong>hotter than infinity</strong> —
    put such a system in contact with any positive-temperature one and energy flows
    <em>out</em> of it. The arithmetic is consistent; the definition earns its keep.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> temperature measures how much heat a body contains —
  that a bathtub of 30 °C water holds less thermal energy than a cup of 90 °C tea. The
  bathtub holds hundreds of times more, because energy content scales with mass while
  temperature is the intensity — the entropy price of each added joule. That distinction
  is exactly what 1/T = ΔS/ΔE encodes: a hot small body is energy-dense but entropy-poor
  per joule, which is why the tea's energy dumps itself eagerly into the bath.</p>

  <h2>The Boltzmann factor: which states get occupied</h2>
  <p>
    Put a small system in contact with a big reservoir at temperature T. The reservoir is
    huge, so its own entropy change when it donates energy E to the small system is
    ΔS ≈ −E/T. The probability of the donation is proportional to the number of reservoir
    microstates consistent with it, and multiplying out the exponentials gives the
    <strong>Boltzmann factor</strong>: the probability of a state with energy E above the
    ground state is proportional to <strong>e^(−E/kT)</strong> — the figure's curve, with
    the normalisation over all states called the <strong>partition function</strong> (the
    denominator Z that turns "proportional to" into "equal to" and from which, remarkably,
    <em>all</em> thermodynamic quantities can be computed by differentiation).
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, kT at room temperature is 1.38 × 10⁻²³ × 293 ≈ 4.0 × 10⁻²¹ J ≈
    0.026 eV — the energy currency of the molecular world. States costing several kT are
    exponentially rare; states costing a fraction of kT are common. Three consequences.
    <strong>Chemistry:</strong> a reaction with activation energy 0.5 eV has rate
    ∝ e^(−0.5/0.026) ≈ 4 × 10⁻⁹ of its barrierless rate at 300 K — but at 310 K the
    factor is e^(−0.5/0.0267) ≈ 7.5 × 10⁻⁹, nearly double. That steepness is the
    Arrhenius "rate doubles per 10 K" rule of thumb, and it is why refrigeration preserves
    food and fevers fight infection. <strong>Atmosphere:</strong> the barometric formula
    P(h) = P₀·e^(−mgh/kT) is the same factor with E = mgh; for air at 288 K the
    <strong>scale height</strong> is mgh/kT ≈ 8.5 km — pressure halves roughly every 6
    km, which is why airliners pressurise and Everest demands bottled oxygen.
    <strong>Evaporation:</strong> the speed distribution's long tail means the fastest
    molecules at a water surface exceed the binding energy and leave, taking
    disproportionate energy with them — the whole machinery of evaporative cooling,
    examined properly in the next section.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the Boltzmann factor turned kT into the molecular currency —
    setting reaction rates, the atmosphere's thickness and evaporation alike. Next it is
    applied at equilibrium itself: how a gas's energy spreads over its degrees of freedom,
    and where the classical accounting visibly breaks.
  </p>

  <h2>Equipartition: where the ideal gas law comes from</h2>
  <p>
    In thermal equilibrium, energy spreads equally: every quadratic degree of freedom
    (½mv² per direction, ½kx² per spring) carries <strong>½kT on average</strong>. A
    monatomic gas (3 translational degrees) therefore stores U = (3/2)NkT of internal
    energy, and combining with pV = NkT (which the same statistics derive from momentum
    transfer at the walls) gives the measured heat capacity of helium, (3/2)R ≈ 12.5
    J/(mol·K). Diatomic molecules add two rotational degrees: heat capacity (5/2)R ≈
    20.8 J/(mol·K) — exactly what nitrogen's tables show at room temperature. But add
    <em>vibration</em> and classical physics over-promises ((7/2)R), while the data show
    vibration "freezing out" below ~1000 K. That failure is historically precious: it is
    the quantum mechanics of the previous lesson asserting itself — vibrational levels
    are spaced more than kT apart, so the Boltzmann factor leaves them empty. The heat
    capacities of gases are, quietly, one of the places quantum mechanics was discovered.
  </p>

  <h2>The Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution and what it explains</h2>
  <p>
    For a gas in thermal equilibrium, the Boltzmann factor applied to kinetic energy
    produces a specific prediction for how molecular speeds are shared: a bell-shaped
    <strong>Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution</strong> whose most probable speed is
    √(2kT/m) and whose rms speed is √(3kT/m) — for nitrogen at 20 °C, about 415 m/s
    and 510 m/s respectively. The shape matters more than the peak: the distribution has
    a long, thin tail, and <em>the tail does the chemistry</em>. Only tail molecules
    exceed a reaction's activation energy; only tail molecules at a liquid's surface
    exceed the binding energy and escape (evaporation — with the escapees taking
    above-average energy, which is why sweating cools you); and only tail molecules
    exceed a planet's escape velocity. That last application is planetary: Earth holds
    nitrogen and oxygen (tail speeds far below the 11.2 km/s escape velocity) but has
    lost nearly all its primordial hydrogen, whose 1.6 km/s most-probable speed puts a
    real fraction of atoms in the escape tail — over geological time, a leak. The Moon
    is smaller and lost everything; Jupiter's escape velocity of 60 km/s keeps even
    hydrogen, which is why the gas giants never shed their envelopes. One distribution,
    scaled by mass and temperature, sorts the atmospheres of the Solar System.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> at a given temperature all the molecules of a gas move
  at "the" speed of the gas — that the rms speed is what every molecule is doing. The
  distribution's width is the point: at room temperature, nitrogen molecules simultaneously
  fly at everything from near zero to well over twice the most probable speed, colliding
  and exchanging energy billions of times a second. Almost everything interesting —
  evaporation, reaction rates, atmospheric escape — happens in the thin tail, not at the
  peak.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: state Boltzmann's entropy formula, say what each symbol measures, and explain why the logarithm is there.</li>
    <li>Application: a reaction's activation energy is 0.5 eV and kT ≈ 0.026 eV at 300 K. Estimate the fraction of collisions energetic enough to react, and say what a 10 K rise does to it.</li>
    <li>Why is the second law described as statistical rather than absolute — and what does that description permit?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) S = k·ln W: S is entropy, W the number of microstates
    consistent with the macrostate, and k Boltzmann's constant, 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K. The
    logarithm makes entropy additive — combine two independent systems and the microstates
    multiply while the entropies add. (2) The fraction is e^(−0.5/0.026) ≈ 4 × 10⁻⁹ of
    collisions; at 310 K it is roughly 7.5 × 10⁻⁹, nearly double — the steepness behind the
    "rate doubles per 10 K" rule, refrigeration and fevers alike. (3) Microscopic dynamics is
    time-reversible; isolated systems drift toward maximal W because essentially all
    microstates live there, not because any force forbids the rest. Fluctuations toward
    lower entropy are therefore allowed — measurable in bacteria-scale motors, absurdly
    unlikely at bathtub scale — making the law overwhelming bookkeeping rather than
    prohibition.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Entropy S = k·ln W counts microstates per macrostate; the second law says isolated systems drift to the macrostate with the most.</li>
    <li>The law is statistical, not absolute: time-reversible dynamics plus astronomical numbers make reversal effectively impossible, never forbidden.</li>
    <li>Temperature is 1/T = ΔS/ΔE — the entropy price of an added joule — which yields the zeroth law and even negative temperatures hotter than infinity.</li>
    <li>The Boltzmann factor e^(−E/kT) sets which states are occupied; kT ≈ 0.026 eV at room temperature is the molecular currency.</li>
    <li>Equipartition gives ½kT per quadratic degree of freedom and the ideal-gas heat capacities — until vibration freezes out and quantum mechanics announces itself.</li>
    <li>The Maxwell–Boltzmann tail does the chemistry: evaporation, reaction rates, and which atmospheres a planet keeps.</li>
    <li>Indistinguishable particles split the statistics into bosons (condensates) and fermions (exclusion, Fermi energy) at low temperature.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lessons carry the counting further. Quantum Foundations supplies
    what the freeze-out and the quantum-statistics sections lean on — discrete energy levels
    and indistinguishable particles. Special Relativity is the ladder's other revision of
    Newtonian mechanics, and Fluid Dynamics extends the same molecular picture to matter in
    motion rather than in equilibrium.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Quantum statistics: when particles are indistinguishable.</strong> The
    counting above treats particles as distinguishable balls. True quantum particles are
    not, and their exchange statistics split the world in two. <strong>Bosons</strong>
    (integer spin: photons, helium-4) happily share a state, and their occupation obeys
    Bose–Einstein statistics — the extreme case being the <strong>Bose–Einstein
    condensate</strong>, predicted in 1924 and created in 1995 (rubidium-87 at 170
    nanokelvin; Nobel 2001), where a macroscopic fraction of the gas collapses into one
    quantum state and becomes a single wavefunction you can photograph.
    <strong>Fermions</strong> (half-integer spin: electrons, protons, helium-3) obey the
    Pauli exclusion principle: one per state, filling levels from the bottom up to the
    <strong>Fermi energy</strong>. This one rule explains why white dwarf and neutron
    stars resist gravity (the compact-objects lesson returns to it), why metals conduct,
    why chemistry has shells, and why helium-3 and helium-4 — chemically identical twins
    — are radically different fluids below a few kelvin. Thermal physics at low
    temperature <em>is</em> quantum statistics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond equilibrium.</strong> Nearly everything above assumes equilibrium or
    near it; the physics of systems driven far from it is younger and partly open. The
    <strong>fluctuation theorems</strong> (1990s) quantified the rare entropy-decreasing
    excursions predicted since Boltzmann, and were verified by dragging single RNA
    molecules and colloids with optical tweezers — the second law holds on average, with
    an exponentially suppressed probability of temporary violation. Jarzynski's equality
    turned nonequilibrium work into equilibrium free-energy differences, a result now
    used in molecular simulation daily. Landauer's principle connects the subject to
    information: erasing one bit of information necessarily dissipates at least kT·ln 2
    of heat — computation is a thermodynamic process, and "Maxwell's demon" was resolved
    not by exorcism but by billing the demon for its memory erasures. The crossover to
    information theory is exact: Shannon's information entropy and Boltzmann's S are the
    same mathematics applied to different state counts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> The arrow of time — why the universe started in such
    an improbably low-entropy state (the past hypothesis) — remains the deepest unsolved
    problem in the field: the second law explains the arrow <em>given</em> that initial
    condition, not the condition itself. Non-equilibrium pattern formation (convection
    cells, turbulence, life itself as entropy-exporting structure) lacks a general theory
    comparable to equilibrium thermodynamics. And at the frontier, the same statistics
    runs cosmology: black-hole entropy (S = k·A/4·(c³/Għ)) is the largest entropy store
    known and the strongest clue we have that gravity, information and thermodynamics are
    one subject — the question the compact-objects and quantum-foundations pages circle
    without landing.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Boltzmann (1877); Feynman, <em>Lectures on Physics</em> Vol. I, ch. 40; Schroeder, <em>An Introduction to Thermal Physics</em>; Anderson et al., <em>Science</em> 269, 198 (1995) — Bose–Einstein condensation. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
